+# Upstream PR 1038 rebase and adversarial audit
+
+The remaining local work was rebased on upstream `219f74aeb7cf77659b7fd4f0e8e9dd03a3a5b38e` after PR 1038 merged. The previous fast-CI run was stopped because it tested the old base; its partial results are not a pass.
+
+## Pointer induction legality
+
+A constant comparison alone does not bound an unsigned counter. A 32-bit counter with stride `1 << 30` and `i != (1 << 30) + 1` wraps without reaching its bound, while pointer induction variables continue advancing. On a sparse 4-GiB mapping the upstream compiler with the pass enabled timed out; the pinned GCC 16.2 oracle and LCCC with the pass disabled both returned the expected result. The regression touches only a few pages but requires sufficient 64-bit virtual address space; a failed mapping or timeout is a test failure, not a skip.
+
+The legality proof checks which comparison edge actually returns to the header, the comparison operation, exact step of the comparison's recurrence increment, source width, signedness, and a conservative overflow-checked bound including the final increment. In particular an inequality bound must be reachable. Signed widening is constrained before the sign bit, avoiding a negative address index. The original scan optimization remains a two-sided code-generation gate: pass-on uses one increment, pass-off retains more. A reduced-width exhaustive proof test covers all comparison operators, both branch orientations and pre/post-increment comparisons. The rewrite also handles a store using the same pointer phi as both address and value; otherwise removing the phi would leave its value use dangling.
+
+## Definition-based nonzero proof
+
+An additional IR audit found that constant-cast resolution masked only the destination width, not the source width: an out-of-range constant interpreted in an 8-bit source could incorrectly be treated as a nonzero 32-bit value. The resolver now interprets each source width and signedness before conversion, handles all 128 bits, and uses a single bounded iterative traversal. The OR-definition walk is likewise iterative and examines both operands, so it cannot recursively exhaust the compiler stack and can discover a proof on either side. Unit fixtures cover source-width truncation, signed extension, high 128-bit bits, either OR operand, and adversarial depth. These are IR-level edge cases; a C-level miscompile has not been demonstrated.
+
+## Toolchains and status
+
+The installed multilib compiler still links and runs 32-bit programs. GCC 14.2 AArch64 and RISC-V cross drivers were privately extracted to coexist with Debian's conflicting multilib packages; both dynamic and static hello programs ran under qemu-user. The first fast-CI attempt exposed that this alone was insufficient: several tests and LCCC itself expect headers, libraries, and dynamic loaders at `/usr/<triplet>` and `/usr/lib/gcc-cross/<triplet>`. The cross libc, development headers, and libgcc development packages were installed with apt's `--no-remove`, preserving gcc-multilib. Each of the four affected TLS, unwinding, and DSO gates then passed separately, including real AArch64 and RISC-V execution. The AArch64 conditional-select regression and all eight RISC-V relaxation combinations also passed. Two 4-GiB swapfiles are active.
+
+The first fast-CI attempt also exposed an audit-census increase from an `unreachable!` in diagnostic-only overflow handling. The fallback now returns an unknown result instead of panicking if a future operation reaches it; the audit ratchet holds without raising its baseline. The source changes and new tests require final exact-tree validation. No green fast-CI pass, complete kernel image, or real guest boot is claimed here. No full/slow CI is run locally.
+
+## Static address/dereference cancellation
+
+The first serial `bzImage` build with the unchanged CachyMod configuration and real ELF objtool reached 1,609 CC/AS entries, then failed in `drivers/tty/sysrq.c:487`: LCCC rejected the valid static initializer `&(*(const struct sysrq_key_op *)((void *)0))`. GCC 16 accepted the reduced reproducer. The kernel configuration was not weakened: `CONFIG_X86_KERNEL_IBT`, `CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR`, `CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_STRONG`, and `CONFIG_UNWINDER_ORC` remain enabled; `.config` SHA-256 is `89968f5b7356960ef904e4288bbc11c505c29745229a5e3f4ec93ec32878ab54`.
+
+In C11 6.5.3.2p3, `&*p` has the value of `p` without dereferencing it. Lowering now handles scalar pointer values and symbolic relocations separately, and can materialize compound literals through `&*` before resolving their addresses. Relocation-aware aggregate lowering also accepts scalar pointer constants and recognizes nested compound literal addresses. The regression executes GCC 16 and LCCC on 32- and 64-bit x86, GNU11/GNU2x, O0/O2, with null and nonzero pointers, function and string addresses, global offsets, arrays, aggregate members, nested/anonymous objects and local statics; it separately requires four nonconstant cases to be rejected. The fixture also passed a Clang 23 x86-64 execution. Existing static-local (94 rows), designator (24), brace-elision (4), and vector static-init (47 objects x 2 levels) gates passed. The first compiler fix revealed two additional aggregate/compound literal failures in the expanded fixture; the current fix addresses them rather than omitting those cases.
+
+With the current frozen compiler, the actual `drivers/tty/sysrq.o` rebuilt successfully using its Kbuild command and the original configuration (`/home/user/kernel-sysrq-s35.log`). Compiler SHA-256 is `10eaaeb0e5b382cad1bf6693b896b28fba24e160c6bbd86ca48034a9c9502c78`. The latest full fast-CI run was stopped after 151 passing gates to change compiler source; it is not a pass for this source. The new source still needs an exact-tree fast-CI run and a full kernel image/guest boot. Full/slow CI remains forbidden locally.
+
+## Red-team follow-up: array values versus addresses
+
+The next exact-tree fast-CI attempt passed the Rust unit suite (7,039 passed, 10 ignored) and 280-plus gates but failed `compound-literal-element`: the initial `&*` fix classified a bare compound literal as an address even when it initialized an entire array member. Two fixtures silently emitted an extra relocation and incorrect values; another was rejected. The run was stopped, not marked green. A red control also exposed the same miscompile when a *different* pointer member legitimately forces relocation-aware aggregate lowering.
+
+The address detector now requires an explicit address of the literal. The shared scalar-array member planner separately recognizes a compound literal of the member's own array type as the entire member's payload, including in a relocation-aware image; it does not turn that value into a relocation. The expanded compound-literal gate now has 41 GCC 16.2 differential cases, including mixed scalar/pointer arrays, struct arrays, member override, actual relocation tables, nested literals, and automatic-storage rejection. It passes all verdict, runtime, and image comparisons. The `&*` matrix also passes, now including a pointer-array literal containing an addressed compound literal.
+
+Additional negative controls exposed a pre-existing sema gap: GCC rejects same-typed array compound literals initializing automatic array members, while LCCC accepted them. A type- and subobject-aware, prefiltered initializer walk now rejects positional and nested designated cases without rejecting array literals that legitimately decay into pointer elements. Four pre-fix controls failed against GCC; the new tests pass. Related static-local, designator, brace-elision and vector initializer gates pass. `cargo fmt --check` and strict Clippy (76 probed policy lints) pass on this source; the Clippy run was focused, not a full fast-CI pass. The exact new source still requires a complete fast-CI rerun and kernel image/boot. The kernel configuration remains unchanged; no local slow/full CI was run.
+
+The corrected compiler was frozen as S36 (compiler SHA-256 `f18a78c3b46639afca7549d0ac8eca923105695fc79c7d1491bb432081d07a75`) and the actual kernel `drivers/tty/sysrq.o` again compiled with Kbuild's original flags; configuration SHA-256 remained `89968f5b7356960ef904e4288bbc11c505c29745229a5e3f4ec93ec32878ab54`. Compiler changes mean previously compiled kernel objects cannot be treated as evidence for this new binary; the full image must be rebuilt using the frozen S36 path.
+
+## Fast CI completed; subsequent rebase and #1039 audit
+
+On head `1483a5838b4229fcfe61a9957f2ec9cd1d19d3ba` over base `219f74aeb7cf77659b7fd4f0e8e9dd03a3a5b38e`, the *complete* local fast CI passed **509 gates, 0 failed, 8 skipped**. Its exact-tree stamp is `474ff865d41b009fee31233f32a3fd87cf224bd4` (fast, Debian 13); cargo-test had 7,039 passed/10 ignored, and rustfmt, MSRV, strict Clippy, the former compound-literal failure, the audit ratchet and linker suite all passed. This green result is archived as S37 and is **not** a CI stamp for subsequent rebases. The preceding disk-full CI failure was stopped, and a new full fast run passed after obsolete generated incremental caches were removed.
+
+A fresh fetch then found upstream merge #1039, `8e7277c4849e231d72c7b273f44ef1f05aee082e`. The changes were rebased on it. The one lane-plan conflict was resolved by keeping upstream's two newly added checks plus the revised pointer-IV measurement; the codegen-a prediction is the exact sum, 405.97 seconds. Rustfmt, diff-check and CI/local parity passed. The new upstream focused recursion-clone, empty-asm barrier, dead stack store and nbody shape checks passed, as did the 41-row compound-literal and 51-row file-scope suites. No full exact-rebased-tree CI has yet passed.
+
+Audit of #1039 found that its alleged constant-evaluation depth regression passed a *volatile* argument to `deep`, so it did not exercise constant interpretation: with IPCP disabled, the old fixture still compiled, returned 100000 and retained a `call deep`. The fixture now passes real constants, requires the IPCP debug trace to show `deep(100)` evaluated to 100, requires `main` itself to retain the `deep(100000)` call, and executes the result; GCC 16 and LCCC return `100 100000`. With IPCP disabled the new positive control fails. With the depth limit temporarily increased from 65,536 to 131,072 in a separate experimental compiler, the direct 100,000-deep call aborted and the new test failed; the source limit was restored unchanged. This is evidence for the regression, not a change to the depth policy.
+
+The rebased compiler was frozen for a real serial Kbuild as compiler SHA-256 `fc28a8672a42ccc1844a73e980a8c1ceb4ec5609e6280571bcb0c92e75f3107d` and linker SHA-256 `1cc8d473c8a08d2359a856e45380f9800bbb829c6738463e80b485cc409dcdf4`. The new image build uses a new compiler path so Kbuild notices changed commands, real ELF objtool and unchanged `.config` (`89968f5b7356960ef904e4288bbc11c505c29745229a5e3f4ec93ec32878ab54`), with stack protector, ORC and IBT retained. The image and actual QEMU boot remain unproved. No local full/slow CI was run.
+
+## Rebase to #1041/#1042, kernel SIP blocker and initial DWARF red control
+
+After the exact S39 fast-CI pass (513 passed, 0 failed, 8 skipped, tree `dd6b42e133450dbd8cd1ad6be9e9262e4dbfaf89`, head `877317b7cbb436496f84b381c9b64883999ac34e`) the untouched-config image build passed `drivers/tty/sysrq.o` but failed at `net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_sip.c` after 2,100-plus CC/AS actions. Sixteen static initializer leaves using a non-null string literal as the conditional test were rejected; GCC 16.2 and Clang 23 accepted and ran the minimized case. The full image and boot did not complete. No configuration option was weakened.
+
+At the user's request the work was saved and rebased on newly merged #1041/#1042, upstream `e6a6cc7e31cfb2df00dd3cd65c2a78f60717a1aa`. Both sides of the history/preprocessor conflicts were retained; the new lane split and updated diagnostic cost have a correct sum, and `check_ci_lane_plan.py` and CI/local parity pass. A preprocessor helper needed its call adapted from the old test-only `scan` method to `scan_part`; the rebased compiler then built. Both multiline-splice and the new upstream comment-bridge GCC differential gates passed, as did null-pointer-policy and kconfig-query controls. The post-rebase tree has not yet passed full fast CI.
+
+The SIP fix recognizes only a direct string literal as a non-null conditional test, separately from its numeric value: sema, static-initializer validation and lowering select the same arm, including pointer relocation initializers. It does not assume that a string address truncated through an integer cast remains nonzero. A red control using the pre-fix compiler rejected the new fixture; GCC and Clang accepted it. The file-scope GCC/LCCC m32/m64, GNU11/GNU2x, O0/O2 executable matrix passes, including pointer relocation values and two nonconstant rejection controls. The actual `net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_sip.o` built with frozen compiler `b15296a6b31a59232497e2f0de07f22ea76158728ecae69c772c54b2e73228a5` and real ELF objtool; `.config` SHA-256 remains `89968f5b7356960ef904e4288bbc11c505c29745229a5e3f4ec93ec32878ab54`, with Stack Protector, ORC and IBT still enabled. A complete image must be rebuilt for the final compiler and booted in QEMU.
+
+Initial DWARF comparison: GCC 16 and Clang 23 compiling a two-line `foo(int x)` at `-O0 -g` let GDB print `x=4` and local `y=7` after stepping; LCCC generated line and subprogram DIEs but GDB reported both symbols missing. Its generated DWARF currently has no formal-parameter, local-variable or base-type DIEs; this is a substantial real feature gap, not evidence of parity or superiority. The previous fast CI's line-table and debug-code-invariance checks passed but do not cover these source-variable capabilities. Further implementation must propagate truthful source types, lifetimes and changing locations through optimization and target backends rather than fabricating locations.
+
+## DWARF line-state correctness (not full source-variable support)
+
+A GNU-as comparison demonstrated that the built-in assemblers silently discarded `.loc` attributes: an input specifying `is_stmt 0`, `prologue_end`, `epilogue_begin`, `basic_block`, ISA and a discriminator produced only plain rows. The old S41 compiler is retained as a red control; the new `check_dwarf_loc_options.py` rejects its x86-64 object because all attributes were missing.
+
+The shared parser and DWARF v4 line writer now carry these six attributes through the x86-64/i686, AArch64 and RISC-V assemblers. Persistent `is_stmt`/ISA values are computed in source order before rows are split into executable sections; the writer resets each sequence to DWARF defaults and emits the needed state transitions. One-row flags and discriminators reset after their row. A multi-byte discriminator is emitted without a per-row allocation. The new test decodes row *semantics*, rather than matching raw opcode bytes, against GNU as for all four targets, including cross-section state, and validates both relocatable objects and GNU-linked images. All four legs pass in this environment; missing optional cross oracles are explicitly skipped rather than reported as passes. The existing multi-object/linker/strip/compressed-input DWARF suite and GDB line-resolution test also pass. Unit tests cover valid options and invalid/missing arguments; full exact-tree CI is still required.
+
+This is a line-table interoperability fix, not a complete DWARF implementation. GNU `.loc view` remains unmodeled, and the earlier GDB red control for source locals/parameters and type DIEs remains unresolved; optimized variable locations, lifetimes, inlined-call scopes and type reconstruction also remain open. There is no evidence of overall quality, performance or usefulness exceeding GCC or LLVM.
+
+## Rebases through #1043, recovery and line-program compactness
+
+The session work was saved and rebased on `origin/main=935f243273329e168aa80a29f57e54bbbd0b64cf` (merge #1043). A later fetch plus `ls-remote` reported the same tip. Recovery after an environment reset restored exact Git history from the verified bundle without replacing the user worktree; six restored worktree files were older than the committed tree, and their differences were saved separately before restoring the committed versions.
+
+The kernel work tree was regenerated from the verified Linux 6.18.58 archive (SHA-256 `069b19f9032c4dac8149ae4b6520c35debca0f42a9f6d52ee55ba6ca9b818013`) and the same 29 package patches. The target is the unmodified x86-64 defconfig from that patched source, not the package's different full config. Reconstructing defconfig with LCCC for CC, AS and LD produced exactly the previous `.config` SHA-256 `89968f5b7356960ef904e4288bbc11c505c29745229a5e3f4ec93ec32878ab54`. A byte-exact copy is persisted as `artifacts/linux-6.18.58-exact.config` in the session workspace. IBT, strong stack protector and ORC remain enabled. A package-config reconstruction that lacked two of these was rejected and never used for a kernel image. The new compiler requires a fresh exact-tree image and real boot; no complete image or QEMU success is claimed.
+
+A first measured x86-64 `.loc` stress fixture (5,000 rows, 5 sequential samples; shared host, not a controlled whole-compiler benchmark) showed GNU as median 8.58 ms and LCCC 37.28 ms, with `.debug_line` sizes 45,532 vs 65,529 bytes. DWARF v4 special opcodes now encode the common address/line delta in one byte when both fit; paths requiring per-row linker-relaxation relocations retain their original relocations. A second run produced 45,533 bytes for LCCC vs 45,532 for GNU as, with semantic flag rows equal; median elapsed times were 35.65 vs 8.90 ms, so this is a size improvement, **not** evidence of a speed win. A positive/mutation check fails the previous compiler because it emits no special opcodes; explicit address/line boundary cases and 1,000 seeded rows match GNU as before and after linking. Existing GNU-as flag comparisons and line-table/linker/GDB checks pass across the four supported architectures. Results are in `artifacts/dwarf-flags-bench-s43.json`, `artifacts/dwarf-flags-bench-s44.json` and `artifacts/focused-s44-fixed.log` in the session workspace. This does not implement source variables, types, lexical scopes or optimized locations; a renewed GDB red control on the rebased tree still reports no symbols `x` or `y`, while GCC 16.2 and Clang 23.1.4 print 4 and 7.
+
+## Fast-CI preprocessor regression and split terminator
+
+The first exact-tree fast run after #1043 was stopped after a real failure: 7,074 unit tests passed, one failed, and ten were ignored. The failing upstream test `a_bridged_ordinary_line_keeps_its_break` exposed an over-conservative rebase integration guard; the earlier targeted compiler regressions did not exercise that Rust assertion. This run is not a fast-CI pass.
+
+The guard now permits a safe break on an ordinary line that entered inside a block comment only when both the physical and splice scanners have seen the comment close, while directive context and all other safety checks still apply. A further diagnostic red control exposed a separate source-position defect when a closing `*/` itself was formed across a splice: GCC 16 diagnoses an undeclared token at line 4 column 2, while the prior LCCC reported line 2 column 10. The splice scanner now carries a pending comment-ending star across physical parts, without letting the `*` of the opening `/*` double as a terminator. The fixed compiler reports line 4 column 2, like GCC. The 32/64-bit GNU11 O0/O2 execution and diagnostic-position controls, C23, 400 seeded comment bridges across five frontends, and the four-target DWARF regressions pass. A complete Rust-unit rerun and exact-tree fast CI are still required before this is gated.
